    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Topic Shift"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 84633,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84633.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "No, you're absolutely right. And we have this bill that this is a bill, of course, that we propose that we're going to try to move called the Clean Future Act, and this is our response to climate, our climate action plan, if you will. But you're absolutely right. If you talk to people about You know, like one of those sci-fi movies where, you know, the Statue of Liberty is going underwater and 100 foot waves are hitting Manhattan, they think you're crazy. And so you have to bring it down to the level where, you know, this affects your health because, you know, people that have, you know, and weather patterns, in other words, that it's a health and safety issue. You know, if you look at the Jersey Shore where I am, you know, we know the sea level is rising, right? And we know that, you know, People's health are being impacted by these greenhouse cases. If you have asthma or you have serious respiratory problems, you're going to have a harder time breathing. So that's how you have to link it. I call it health and safety issues, not catastrophic issues.",
